<article xml:id="week4" label="0004"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 151 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
     For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
    </p>
  </introduction>
<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #4 (Sections 2.6, 2.7, &amp; 2.8, Exam I Review)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you. The Exam I review begins in the 'Now Grades Go Up' section.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
      <exercise><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the graph below to complete the following tasks.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Draw the secant line passing through the points <m>(-2,2)</m> and <m>(1,-1)</m>. What is its slope?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>m=-1</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Draw the tangent line passing through the point <m>(1,-1)</m>. What is its slope, approximately?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f'(1) \approx -1</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Why do you think these two values are similar?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
                Think about the definition of the slope of the secant line between two points, <m>(a,f(a))</m> and <m>(a+h,f(a+h))</m>, separated by <m>h</m> units.
                Compare this to the definition of the slope of the tangent line between two points.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              For relatively small values of <m>h</m>, in this case <m>h=3</m>, <m>f'(1) = \displaystyle\lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} \approx \dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} = \dfrac{f(4)-f(1)}{3} = -1</m>.
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
            <p>
              For the graph below, indicate the points where <m>f(x)</m> is not differentiable. Then, categorize each as a jump discontinuity, a removable discontinuity (hole), a vertical asymptote, cusp, corner, or vertical tangent.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              
            </p>
          </answer>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            The following questions address the continuity of piecewise functions.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the value(s) of <m>A</m> that make <m>f(x) = \begin{cases}\sqrt{Ax+5} \amp x \ge 2\\ 3x-A-1 \amp x \lt 1\end{cases}</m> continuous everywhere on its domain.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>A=2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the value(s) of <m>B</m> that make <m>g(x) = \begin{cases}7x^3-2x^2+Bx+1 \amp x \le 2\\ \frac{2-Bx}{x^2} \amp x \gt 1\end{cases}</m> continuous everywhere on its domain.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>B=-2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT) to answer the following questions.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Does the interval <m>(-2,0)</m> contain a root to the function <m>f(x)=x^2-4x+2</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Possibly, but not guaranteed by the IVT
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Does the interval <m>(0,1)</m> contain a root to the function <m>f(x)=x^2-4x+2</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Yes, by the IVT
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Does the interval <m>(1,3)</m> contain a root to the function <m>f(x)=x^2-4x+2</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Possibly, but not guaranteed by the IVT
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Does the interval <m>(3,4)</m> contain a root to the function <m>f(x)=x^2-4x+2</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              Yes, by the IVT
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Evaluate the following limits algebraically. If a limit does not exist, and is not <m>\pm \infty</m>, write DNE. Do not use L'Hospital's rule.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\dfrac{x^2+3x-7}{x^3+2x+8}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>0</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow-\infty}\dfrac{4x^2-x+5}{7+x^2}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>4</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\arctan\left(\dfrac{2x^4+1}{x^3+5x-1}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\dfrac{9+3x^2}{\sqrt{x^4+5}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>3</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{x^6+2x^2-1}}{x^2-x+10}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\infty</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow-\infty}\dfrac{2e^{4x}-5e^{-5x}}{7e^{-5x}+3e^{4x}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>-\dfrac{5}{7}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\dfrac{-6^{2x}+2(0.2)^{3x}}{-4(0.1)^{4x}+5 \cdot 6^{2x}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>-\dfrac{1}{5}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\left(\sqrt{4x^4+4x^2}-\sqrt{4x^4-2x}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>1</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x\rightarrow\infty}\left(\ln(2x^3+4x)-\ln(x^3+x^2)\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\ln(2)</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Identify the horizontal asymptotes for the following functions.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x)=\dfrac{\sqrt{9x^4-2x+4}}{x^2+4x-6}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=3</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>g(x)=\dfrac{-2e^{8x}+6e^{-x}}{3e^{8x}-8e^{-x}}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=-\dfrac{2}{3}</m>, <m>y=-\dfrac{3}{4}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h(x)=\arctan\left(\dfrac{2x^3-7x+4}{4x^2+2x-7}\right)</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y=\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>, <m>y=-\dfrac{\pi}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Find the average rate of change of the following functions on the given intervals.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x)=x^3-3x^2+2x</m> on <m>[0,2]</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>m=0</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>g(x)=\sqrt{x-2}</m> on <m>[2,6]</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>m=\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h(x)=\dfrac{4}{x}</m> on <m>[2,4]</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>m=-\dfrac{1}{2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Complete the following tasks to find the instantaneous rate of change of <m>f(x)=x^2+2x-1</m> at <m>x=1</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>f(1+h)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f(1+h)=h^2+4h+2</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>f(1+h)-f(1)</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f(1+h)=(h^2+4h+2)-(1^2+2-1)=h^2+4h</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} = \dfrac{h^2+4h}{h} = h+4</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Compute <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\dfrac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} = \lim_{h\rightarrow 0}(h+4)=4</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the limit definition of the derivative, <m>f'(x)=\displaystyle\lim_{h\rightarrow 0}\dfrac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}</m>, to find the derivatives of the following functions. Do not use any derivative rules from outside this section.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x)=x^2-3x+4</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>f'(x)=2x-3</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>g(t)=\sqrt{t+2}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>g'(t)=\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{t+2}}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>h(\theta)=\dfrac{5}{\theta}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>h'(\theta)=-\dfrac{5}{\theta^2}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Suppose that the displacement of a particle, measured in centimeters, is given by <m>s(t)=5t^2-t+4</m> with <m>t\geq 0</m> measured in seconds.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the average velocity of the particle on the interval <m>[0,1]</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
                Average velocity is the average rate of change of displacement.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>v_{\text{average}}=m=4\frac{\text{cm}}{\text{s}}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the instantaneous velocity of the particle at <m>t=1</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
          <hint>
            <p>
                Instantaneous velocity is the derivative of displacement.
            </p>
          </hint>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>v_{\text{instantaneous}}(t=1)=s'(1)=9\frac{\text{cm}}{\text{s}}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Find the equation of the line tangent to the functions at the given points.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x)=2x^2+3x-1</m> at <m>x=-2</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y-1=-5(x+2)</m> or <m>y=-5x-9</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>f(x)=\sqrt{5-x}</m> at <m>x=1</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
          <answer>
            <p>
              <m>y-2=-\dfrac{1}{4}(x-1)</m> or <m>y=-\dfrac{1}{4}x+\dfrac{9}{4}</m>
            </p>
          </answer>
        </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Simplify <m>\tan(\arcsin(-2x))</m> to an algebraic expression involving <m>x</m> only.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{1+4x^2}{2x}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{1-4x^2}}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2x}{\sqrt{1-4x^2}}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{2x}{1+4x^2}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{\sqrt{1-4x^2}}{2x}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>\mathbf{a}=3\mathbf{i}+4\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}=12\mathbf{i}-5\mathbf{j}</m>, find a unit vector perpendicular to the vector <m>\mathbf{v}=5\mathbf{a}+4\mathbf{b}</m>, <m>\widehat{\mathbf{v}^{\perp}}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{i}-\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{4}{\sqrt{41}}\mathbf{i}+\dfrac{5}{\sqrt{41}}\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\mathbf{j}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{i}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-4\mathbf{a}+5\mathbf{b}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="3"><!-- #3 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>\mathbf{a}=3\mathbf{i}+4\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}=12\mathbf{i}-5\mathbf{j}</m>, find the projection of <m>\mathbf{b}</m> onto <m>\mathbf{a}</m>, <m>\text{proj}_{\mathbf{a}}(\mathbf{b})</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 12,-5\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\langle 3,4\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle \dfrac{16}{25},\dfrac{16}{25}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle \dfrac{64}{25},\dfrac{48}{25}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\left\langle \dfrac{48}{25},\dfrac{64}{25}\right\rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>\mathbf{a}=3\mathbf{i}+4\mathbf{j}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}=12\mathbf{i}-5\mathbf{j}</m>, find the exact value of the cosine of the angle between the vectors <m>\mathbf{a}</m> and <m>\mathbf{b}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{36}{65}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{16}{65}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{16}{25}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{20}{65}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{65}{16}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the shortest distance from the point <m>(3,1)</m> to the line <m>2x+y=5</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>-\dfrac{2}{5}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{5}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2\sqrt{5}}{5}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\dfrac{2}{5}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="6"><!-- #6 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Two forces act on an object. The first force, <m>\mathbf{F}_1</m>, is of magnitude <m>10\text{N}</m> at an angle of <m>30^\circ</m> counterclockwise from the positive
            <m>x</m>-axis. The second force, <m>\mathbf{F}_2</m>, is of magnitude <m>20\text{N}</m> at an angle of <m>150^\circ</m> counterclockwise from the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
            What is the magnitude of the resultant force vector?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>5\sqrt{2}\text{N}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>5\sqrt{3}\text{N}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>10\sqrt{2}\text{N}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>10\sqrt{3}\text{N}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>30\text{N}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            In the previous problem, what angle does the resultant force vector make with respect to the positive <m>x</m>-axis, measured counterclockwise?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>30^\circ</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>60^\circ</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>120^\circ</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>150^\circ</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>180^\circ</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (C)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="8"><!-- #8 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            A box is being pulled <m>0.5\text{m}</m> up an inclined plane by a rope with a force of <m>100\text{N}</m> at an angle of <m>30</m> degrees, measured counterclockwise with respect to the direction of motion of the box. How much work does the force do on the box?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>25\text{J}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>25\sqrt{3}\text{J}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>50\text{J}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>50\sqrt{3}\text{J}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>100\text{J}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (B)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Describe the motion of a particle whose motion is subject to the function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle 5-3\sin(t),1+2\cos(t)\rangle</m> as <m>t</m> increases.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Clockwise around a circle
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Counterclockwise around a circle
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Clockwise around an ellipse
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Counterclockwise around an ellipse
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of these
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (D)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="10"><!-- #10 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Find the vector equation for a line parallel to <m>\langle -2,7 \rangle</m> and passing through the point <m>(3,-2)</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle -2-2t,3+7t \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle 3+2t,-2+7t \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle -2-7t,3+2t \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle 3-7t,-2-2t \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle 3-2t,-2+7t \rangle</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            (E)
          </p>
        </answer>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="11"><!-- #11 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Eliminate the parameter <m>t</m> and simplify the parametric function <m>\langle t^2+1,2t-4 \rangle</m> to Cartesian form.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(y+4)^2=4x</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>(y+4)^2=4(x-1)</m>
